-The PHP project published GHSA-85c2-q967-79q5 on 2026-05-07 disclosing a CWE-416 use-after-free in the ext-soap object-deduplication path ([PHP GHSA-85c2-q967-79q5](https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-85c2-q967-79q5)). The bug lives in the libxml2-node-keyed `SOAP_GLOBAL(ref_map)` hash that `soap_add_xml_ref()` populates when deserialising a SOAP envelope's references; the helper stores raw PHP object pointers without incrementing reference counts. A SOAP envelope carrying an `apache:Map` node with duplicate keys causes the second hash insertion to free the original PHP object while a stale pointer remains; subsequent `href` resolutions return the freed memory address, which the allocator may have already filled with attacker-controlled bytes. php.watch confirms the 2026-05-07 release date and the CVE-to-GHSA mapping ([php.watch — PHP 8.5.6 release, 2026-05-07](https://php.watch/versions/8.5/releases/8.5.6)). Affected versions are PHP 8.2.0 through 8.5.5; fixes shipped in 8.2.31 / 8.3.31 / 8.4.21 / 8.5.6 ([PHP 8 ChangeLog](https://www.php.net/ChangeLog-8.php)). Severity labels split between primaries: GHSA-85c2-q967-79q5 labels severity "High"; NVD's CVSS 4.0 vector for CVE-2026-6722 scores 9.5, which the CVSS-4.0 rubric classifies as Critical. The same release fixes companion memory-management defects CVE-2026-7261 (UAF in `SOAP_PERSISTENCE_SESSION` header parsing — [GHSA-m33r-qmcv-p97q](https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-m33r-qmcv-p97q), CVSS 4.0 6.3 Moderate) and CVE-2026-7262 (NULL dereference in Apache map NULL check — [GHSA-hmxp-6pc4-f3vv](https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-hmxp-6pc4-f3vv), CVSS 4.0 6.3 Moderate). No public proof-of-concept and no in-the-wild exploitation are reported as of this run; the CVSS-4.0 score is 9.5 because a `SoapServer` exposed on a public endpoint is reachable without authentication (SOAP endpoints typically do not require session cookies) and the impact is arbitrary code execution as the PHP worker.
+PHP fixed three flaws in its SOAP extension in releases 8.2.31, 8.3.31, 8.4.21 and 8.5.6 on 2026-05-07 ([PHP ChangeLog, 2026-05-07](https://www.php.net/ChangeLog-8.php); [php.watch, 2026-05-07](https://php.watch/versions/8.5/releases/8.5.6)). CVE-2026-6722 is a use-after-free: when a SOAP envelope carries an `apache:Map` with duplicate keys, the extension's reference map keeps a stale pointer to a freed PHP object, and PHP says an attacker who controls the freed memory can reach remote code execution through `SoapServer::handle` processing an attacker-supplied envelope ([PHP, 2026-05-07](https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-85c2-q967-79q5)). CVE-2026-7261 is a second use-after-free that a SoapServer persisting its handler object in the session (`SOAP_PERSISTENCE_SESSION`) reaches when a header handler returns false or throws; PHP calls control of the freed memory unlikely but not impossible ([PHP, 2026-05-07](https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-m33r-qmcv-p97q)). CVE-2026-7262 is a NULL dereference in the `apache:Map` decoder on a server with a typemap configured, which crashes the process ([PHP, 2026-05-07](https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-hmxp-6pc4-f3vv)). The advisory for CVE-2026-6722 includes a reproducer that triggers the flaw ([PHP, 2026-05-07](https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-85c2-q967-79q5)), and none of the three is listed in CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog as of 2026-10-16 ([CISA KEV catalog, 2026-10-16](https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json)).
+
+**Exposure:** PHP applications that pass untrusted input to a `SoapServer` on 8.2 before 8.2.31, 8.3 before 8.3.31, 8.4 before 8.4.21 or 8.5 before 8.5.6; the 2026-05-07 releases cover only those four branches ([PHP ChangeLog, 2026-05-07](https://www.php.net/ChangeLog-8.php)).
 
-Inclusion is discretionary under PD-11: NVD CVSS 4.0 records the severity as 9.5 (Critical) on a pre-auth network-reachable code path of a runtime present in essentially every Internet-exposed PHP application; the GHSA primary labels severity High. No public proof-of-concept has been released and no in-the-wild exploitation has been reported. The inclusion gate "ENISA EUVD entry with CVSS 9.0–10.0" applies in spirit (ENISA EUVD API returned empty body across every bridge subcommand this run; included for forward-looking patch prioritisation given the breadth of the attack surface. § 5 covers detection / hardening.
+**Detection:** the NULL-dereference flaw shows as PHP worker processes crashing on a segmentation fault while handling SOAP requests ([PHP, 2026-05-07](https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-hmxp-6pc4-f3vv)), visible in the web server or PHP process-manager error log; the advisories describe no post-exploitation behaviour.
 
+**Defender takeaway:** where a SOAP endpoint is exposed, confirm the PHP build is at or above the fixed release for its branch, and move installations on branches older than 8.2 to a supported one.
 
