-Microsoft shipped roughly 120 CVE fixes in the May 2026 cumulative updates (source counts vary 118–138 depending on whether developer-tools and Azure-only items are included); ZDI counts ~30 Critical, none under active exploitation at release ([Tenable, 2026-05-12](https://www.tenable.com/blog/microsofts-may-2026-patch-tuesday-addresses-118-cves-cve-2026-41103); [Krebs on Security, 2026-05-12](https://krebsonsecurity.com/2026/05/patch-tuesday-may-2026-edition/); [ZDI, 2026-05-12](https://www.thezdi.com/blog/2026/5/12/the-may-2026-security-update-review)). **CVE-2026-41089** (Windows Netlogon, CVSS 9.8, CWE-121 stack buffer overflow): unauthenticated remote attacker over the network reaches the domain-controller Netlogon RPC endpoint; Microsoft marks "Exploitation Less Likely" but ZDI flags the pattern as wormable-candidate. **CVE-2026-41096** (Windows DNS Client, CVSS 9.8, CWE-122 heap overflow in `dnsapi.dll`): a crafted DNS response from a MitM or rogue resolver yields code execution as `NetworkService` on every Windows host; defender exposure is anywhere a host might receive an attacker-influenced DNS reply. **CVE-2026-41103** (Microsoft SSO Plugin for Jira/Confluence, CVSS 9.1, "Exploitation More Likely"): unauthenticated attacker forges an Entra ID credential to sign in to self-managed Atlassian; affects public-sector DevSecOps stacks using Microsoft's Entra-ID auth plugin. **CVE-2026-42898** (Dynamics 365 On-Premises, CVSS 9.9): authenticated code injection with scope change — a rare privilege-boundary violation in this product family. Four Microsoft Word RCEs (CVE-2026-40361 / CVE-2026-40364 / CVE-2026-40366 / CVE-2026-40367, CVSS 8.4 each) have the Preview Pane as an attack vector and two are rated "Exploitation More Likely". MITRE ATT&CK mappings: `T1210 Exploitation of Remote Services` (Netlogon), `T1071.004 Application Layer Protocol: DNS` (DNS Client), `T1078.004 Cloud Accounts` (Entra forgery). Detection concepts: monitor Netlogon authentication-pattern anomalies (`4624 Logon Type 3` to DCs from unexpected internal sources, paired with `4769` ticket-request anomalies); alert on outbound DNS to non-corporate resolvers from DC and member hosts; audit Atlassian SSO plugin version inventory; disable Outlook Preview Pane as an interim mitigation for Word RCEs. Hardening: prioritise DCs first (Netlogon is on the DC boundary); inventory `dnsapi.dll` patch state across the fleet; inventory self-managed Atlassian deployments and apply the SSO plugin update before the next work week.
+Microsoft's May 2026 security updates, released on 2026-05-12, fixed CVE-2026-41089, a stack-based buffer overflow in the Windows Netlogon service on domain controllers that an unauthenticated attacker can reach over the network (CVSS 9.8), which Microsoft rated "Exploitation Less Likely" and did not mark exploited ([Microsoft MSRC, 2026-05-12](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/en-US/vulnerability/CVE-2026-41089)). ZDI called the Netlogon flaw wormable ([ZDI, 2026-05-12](https://www.thezdi.com/blog/2026/5/12/the-may-2026-security-update-review)). The same release fixed three further critical flaws: CVE-2026-41096 in the Windows DNS Client (CVSS 9.8), CVE-2026-41103, an elevation of privilege in the Microsoft SSO Plugin for Jira and Confluence (CVSS 9.1), and CVE-2026-42898, code execution in Dynamics 365 on-premises that needs an authenticated attacker (CVSS 9.9) ([Tenable, 2026-05-12](https://www.tenable.com/blog/microsofts-may-2026-patch-tuesday-addresses-118-cves-cve-2026-41103)).
+
+On 2026-05-29 Belgium's Centre for Cybersecurity updated its May Patch Tuesday warning to say the Netlogon flaw is now actively exploited in the wild ([Centre for Cybersecurity Belgium, 2026-05-29](https://ccb.belgium.be/advisories/warning-microsoft-patch-tuesday-may-2026-patches-118-vulnerabilities-16-critical-102)). BleepingComputer reported that the CCB's information came from trusted partners without detail, and that Microsoft said it has no evidence supporting the claim ([BleepingComputer, 2026-06-01](https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/microsoft/critical-windows-netlogon-remote-code-execution-flaw-now-exploited-in-attacks/)). CERT-EU relayed the CCB statement on 2026-06-10 and listed the fixed builds for Windows Server 2012 through 2025 ([CERT-EU, 2026-06-10](https://cert.europa.eu/publications/security-advisories/2026-007/)).
+
+**Exposure:** every Windows domain controller on a Windows Server release from 2012 to 2025 below the May 2026 build listed for its version ([CERT-EU, 2026-06-10](https://cert.europa.eu/publications/security-advisories/2026-007/)).
+
+**Detection:** Netlogon or LSASS crashes and unplanned restarts of domain controllers, and directory traffic to domain controllers from hosts that are not domain members.
+
+**Defender takeaway:** confirm every domain controller runs at least the May 2026 build for its release; the exploitation report is disputed, but the flaw needs no authentication and sits on the domain controller.
 
