-Arctic Wolf Labs published technical evidence on 2026-05-27 of an [in-the-wild campaign abusing CVE-2026-35616](https://arcticwolf.com/resources/blog/forticlient-ems-exploited-via-cve-2026-35616-to-deliver-ekz-infostealer-disguised-as-a-fortinet-patch/), the [CWE-284 improper-access-control flaw in Fortinet FortiClient EMS 7.4.5 and 7.4.6](https://fortiguard.fortinet.com/psirt/FG-IR-26-099) (CVSS 9.1; on [CISA KEV since 2026-04-06](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-35616)). The vulnerable code path trusts the `X-SSL-CLIENT-VERIFY` HTTP header set by a fronting reverse proxy or load balancer instead of validating client-certificate state itself; an unauthenticated attacker on the network spoofs the header to reach privileged management APIs. In the observed campaign, attackers modify Remote Access Profile configurations to push a PowerShell payload signed under the trusted `fortitray.exe` binary that fetches `FortiEndpoint_Patch.exe` — actually the EKZ Infostealer. EKZ copies itself into Chromium/Gecko browser-profile directories (Chrome, Microsoft Edge, Firefox, LibreWolf, Waterfox, Pale Moon, Thunderbird) to clear elevation-validation checks, then dumps encrypted credential and cookie stores via `nss3.dll`. Compromise of a single EMS server cascades to every managed endpoint. Patch is FortiClient EMS 7.4.7.
+Fortinet published CVE-2026-35616 on 2026-04-04, an improper-access-control flaw that bypasses API authentication and authorization in FortiClient EMS 7.4.5 and 7.4.6, marked it known exploited, and fixed it in 7.4.7 and in hotfixes for both affected builds; 7.2 and 8.0 are not affected ([Fortinet PSIRT, 2026-04-04](https://fortiguard.fortinet.com/psirt/FG-IR-26-099)). CISA added it to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on 2026-04-06 ([CISA KEV catalog, 2026-04-06](https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json)). Bishop Fox traced the cause to EMS's Django middleware accepting client-certificate information both from the trusted values Apache's TLS module sets and from client-supplied HTTP headers, which Apache was not configured to strip, so a request can claim a verified client certificate it never presented ([Bishop Fox, 2026-04-07](https://bishopfox.com/blog/api-authentication-bypass-in-forticlient-ems-7-4-5-7-4-6-cve-2026-35616)). Arctic Wolf reported on 2026-05-27 that attackers used the flaw to edit an EMS remote-access profile and insert a script that runs when a managed FortiClient establishes its VPN tunnel: `fortitray.exe` or `ipsec.exe` started `cmd.exe`, which ran an encoded PowerShell command leading to `FortiEndpoint_Patch.exe`, the EKZ infostealer, which steals Chromium and Gecko browser credentials and cookies; PowerShell sent the stolen data out over HTTP POST ([Arctic Wolf, 2026-05-27](https://arcticwolf.com/resources/blog/forticlient-ems-exploited-via-cve-2026-35616-to-deliver-ekz-infostealer-disguised-as-a-fortinet-patch/)). Arctic Wolf notes that every managed endpoint became a potential execution target without a separate intrusion into each device ([Arctic Wolf, 2026-05-27](https://arcticwolf.com/resources/blog/forticlient-ems-exploited-via-cve-2026-35616-to-deliver-ekz-infostealer-disguised-as-a-fortinet-patch/)).
+
+**Exposure:** FortiClient EMS 7.4.5 or 7.4.6 without the hotfix or the 7.4.7 upgrade, and every endpoint such a server manages ([Fortinet PSIRT, 2026-04-04](https://fortiguard.fortinet.com/psirt/FG-IR-26-099)).
+
+**Detection:** on endpoints, `fortitray.exe` or `ipsec.exe` spawning `cmd.exe` and then PowerShell with an encoded command at VPN connect, and a new executable named as a Fortinet patch; on the EMS server, remote-access profile or endpoint-policy changes that add an on-connect script ([Arctic Wolf, 2026-05-27](https://arcticwolf.com/resources/blog/forticlient-ems-exploited-via-cve-2026-35616-to-deliver-ekz-infostealer-disguised-as-a-fortinet-patch/)).
+
+**Triage:** an on-connect script an administrator deployed through change control is expected; one that appeared without a matching change, or a child process chain from the VPN client to PowerShell, is the signal.
+
+**Defender takeaway:** on an EMS server that ran 7.4.5 or 7.4.6 unpatched, review every remote-access profile and endpoint policy for scripts nobody approved, and treat browser credentials and session cookies on endpoints that connected through it as exposed.
+
+## Correction — 2026-10-18T14:08:31Z
 
-**Why it matters to us:** FortiClient EMS is widely deployed across Swiss federal and cantonal network-security estates and across EU public-sector networks. Deep-dive treatment in § 5 below.
+This entry was rewritten from the vendor, researcher and incident sources and merged with a duplicate entry on the same campaign. The flaw is not a header injected through a fronting reverse proxy: Bishop Fox shows EMS's Django middleware accepting client-certificate information from client-supplied headers that Apache was not configured to strip ([Bishop Fox, 2026-04-07](https://bishopfox.com/blog/api-authentication-bypass-in-forticlient-ems-7-4-5-7-4-6-cve-2026-35616)). Arctic Wolf describes the delivery as a VPN on-connect script run by `fortitray.exe` or `ipsec.exe` through `cmd.exe` and PowerShell, and does not report the stealer copying itself into browser profile directories or arriving through an EMS update task ([Arctic Wolf, 2026-05-27](https://arcticwolf.com/resources/blog/forticlient-ems-exploited-via-cve-2026-35616-to-deliver-ekz-infostealer-disguised-as-a-fortinet-patch/)). The CVSS 3.1 base score is 9.8, with 9.1 the temporal score Fortinet also shows ([Fortinet PSIRT, 2026-04-04](https://fortiguard.fortinet.com/psirt/FG-IR-26-099)). No source states how widely EMS is deployed in Switzerland, and the priority moves from high to notable because the fix shipped in April.
