+## Correction — 2026-10-18T14:11:47Z
 
-The chain: authentication bypass via `CVE-2026-20182`/`CVE-2026-20127` (rogue peering connection), then privilege escalation via `CVE-2026-20245` — a malicious `evil_tenant.csv` uploaded through the `request tenant-upload` CLI carries unsanitised shell commands that append a `troot` root user to `/etc/passwd` and `/etc/shadow`, after which the actor reverts configuration changes and deletes the file for anti-forensics. This gives defenders concrete hunts the earlier advisory could not: search SD-WAN Manager instances for unexpected `/etc/passwd` additions, `evil_tenant.csv` artefacts, and `request tenant-upload` execution in CLI logs.
+This entry was rewritten from the vendor and incident sources and merged with a duplicate entry. It said no patch existed; Cisco's advisory lists first fixed releases 20.9.9.2, 20.12.7.2, 20.15.4.5, 20.15.5.3, 20.18.3.1 and 26.1.1.2 ([Cisco, 2026-06-04](https://sec.cloudapps.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-sdwan-privesc-4uxFrdzx)). The flaw is an authenticated, local privilege escalation, not an unauthenticated bypass ([Cisco, 2026-06-04](https://sec.cloudapps.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-sdwan-privesc-4uxFrdzx)), and CISA listed it as exploited on 2026-06-09 ([CISA KEV catalog, 2026-06-09](https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json)). Mandiant dates the zero-day use to April 2026 at a service provider, calls the link to CVE-2026-20127 or CVE-2026-20182 only possible, and describes the attacker deleting the files it created rather than logs ([Mandiant, 2026-06-24](https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/zero-day-exploitation-cisco-catalyst-sd-wan-manager)). The priority moves from critical to notable because fixes have been available since June.
